<Layout title="Evidence, provenance and qualified mappings" description="How attributed evidence and competency questions become traceable SPDTF candidates, and how governed cross-context mappings separate SKOS assertions from mapping-record provenance.">
  <PageMeta category="Understand ontologies" variant="brand" updated="Category 8 mapping method · current implementation remains empty" />
  <h1>Evidence, provenance and qualified mappings</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Every important candidate meaning should be traceable to a question, evidence,
    proposal, review and disposition. “Mapping” is always qualified because different
    records connect different objects and carry different authority.
  </p>

  <h2 id="questions">Competency questions organise evidence</h2>
  <p>
    Evidence is collected to answer a scoped business question, not simply because a file
    exists. A good competency question identifies the context, expected result and why the
    answer matters. Evidence may support, contradict or leave the question unresolved.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Example question-to-evidence record</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Question</th><td>Which source supports the legal title associated with this property?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Context owner</th><td>Conveyancing, with a Property Data Services source dependency.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Evidence</th><td>Participant scenario, title record, source metadata and PDTF schema comparison.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Candidate impact</th><td>Legal title resource, property relationship and source-evidence relationship.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Open issue</th><td>Whether the source identifies the title assertion or only supplies a document.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Test</th><td>A competency query can retrieve the property, title and attributed source.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="traceability">Traceability from source to version</h2>
  <Diagram size="large" caption="A traceability receipt binds an attributed source to the question it informs, the exact candidate construct, its review and disposition, and the immutable version that includes the decision.">
flowchart LR
  accTitle: Evidence traceability receipt for a semantic candidate
  accDescr: An attributed source supports a competency question, which links to an exact candidate construct, review record, disposition and immutable candidate version.
  Source["Attributed source"]:::external
  Question["Competency question"]:::user
  Construct["Candidate construct"]:::process
  Review["Review and dissent"]:::user
  Disposition["Recorded disposition"]:::security
  Version["Immutable candidate version"]:::service
  Source --> Question
  Question --> Construct
  Construct --> Review
  Review --> Disposition
  Disposition --> Version
  </Diagram>
  <p>
    Machine extraction is recorded as a derivation, not as the source authority. If
    evidence conflicts, preserve the competing claims and ask the owner to dispose them.
    Do not manufacture consensus or collapse a source document, its claim and the
    real-world thing it describes into one resource.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mapping">Five qualified mapping meanings</h2>
  <table>
    <caption>These records must not share an unqualified “mapping” label</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Qualified meaning</th><th scope="col">What it connects</th><th scope="col">Standing</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">PDTF schema RML schema–ontology verification</th><td>Published schema structures and the schema-derived ontology.</td><td>Retained implementation and traceability evidence.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Legacy JSON-LD context mapping</th><td>Legacy JSON property names and ontology terms for compatibility views.</td><td>Planned or retained migration work; not new semantic authority.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">SPDTF cross-context semantic mapping</th><td>Related but non-identical meanings at bounded-context boundaries.</td><td>Candidate agreement owned by Interoperability and reviewed by affected groups.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Property Pack source-item coverage link</th><td>One of 451 required source items and one or more candidate constructs.</td><td>Coverage evidence only; <strong>not RML or R2RML</strong>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Deployment transformation runtime</th><td>Operational representations where a deployment needs a transform.</td><td>Optional implementation service; cannot decide semantic meaning.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="cross-context-mappings">Category 8: cross-context mappings</h2>
  <p>
    Category 8 in the ODR-0071 modelling-category lineage is the cross-cutting bridge
    between two or more bounded contexts. It contains correspondence decisions, not the
    domain resources owned by either context. The sibling semantic-modelling record
    <a href="https://github.com/hm-group/semantic-modelling/blob/0eb7c59b4dbb24b9707e5dd956ab34668b093141/docs/ontology/odr/ODR-0071h-cat8-cross-domain-mappings-pipeline-adoption.md"><cite>ODR-0071h — Cat 8 Cross-Domain Mappings: Pipeline Adoption Decisions</cite></a>
    is useful prior art for its human decision lifecycle; current SPDTF authority comes from
    <a href="/modelling/adr/adr-0063">ADR-0063</a> and
    <a href="/modelling/adr/adr-0067">ADR-0067</a>, not from silently inheriting that
    sibling implementation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Keep three mechanisms separate. A <a href="/semantic-modelling/bounded-contexts">context
    map</a> records architectural relationships and responsibilities. The Common boundary
    contains a deliberately small set of shared exchange meanings. A term-level mapping
    keeps two meanings in their own semantic homes and records a reviewed relationship
    between them. A context-map arrow is therefore not, by itself, a mapping assertion.
  </p>
  <div class="callout callout--warn">
    <p class="callout__label">Method contract—not current implementation</p>
    <p>
      Property Pack 0.1 emits SKOS schemes, concepts, labels, definitions, notations and
      scheme membership. It emits no SKOS hierarchy or mapping predicates. Its
      <a href="/spdtf/property-pack/relationships#cross-context">cross-context mapping register</a>
      is empty. SSSOM and SEMAPV remain deferred
      candidates: no SSSOM version or profile has been selected, and no SSSOM artefact or
      conformance receipt exists.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3 id="skos-and-sssom">SKOS says what; SSSOM can record why</h3>
  <p>
    A reviewed SKOS triple states the relationship. SSSOM can carry a triple's sources,
    versions, justification, authorship and review metadata without changing the predicate,
    but SPDTF has not selected it for internal cross-context records. SSSOM does not decide
    or prove the relationship, and it can carry predicates other than SKOS. For a SKOS
    mapping, both endpoints are SKOS concepts, conventionally in different concept schemes.
    Mapping OWL classes or properties needs a separately governed predicate and profile.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Layer</th><th scope="col">What it records</th><th scope="col">SPDTF standing</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/skos-reference/#mapping">SKOS mapping assertion</a></th><td>Subject concept, one reviewed mapping predicate and object concept.</td><td>Accepted bounded target where justified; no mapping predicate is emitted today.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://mapping-commons.github.io/sssom/1.0/Mapping/">SSSOM mapping record</a></th><td>For the concept-to-concept records described here, the four core fields are <code>subject_id</code>, <code>predicate_id</code>, <code>object_id</code> and <code>mapping_justification</code>. Only an endpoint explicitly typed <code>rdfs:Literal</code> may omit its ID, in which case its label carries the literal. SSSOM can also carry sources, versions, author, reviewer, date, confidence, comment and tool metadata.</td><td>Deferred candidate for a named external-vocabulary use case; not selected for internal cross-context records and no SPDTF profile exists.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Governed mapping set</th><td>Version, scope, licence, owners, review state, individual records and validation receipt.</td><td>Required before a mapping set is promoted or represented as governed or release-candidate material. Isolated proposals may be published for review only with explicit candidate status and no implication of semantic approval. The current Property Pack register is empty.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    SSSOM uses controlled <a href="https://mapping-commons.github.io/sssom/1.0/Mapping/">mapping-justification</a>
    values from SEMAPV. Confidence may help reviewers prioritise work, but it never replaces
    justification or human approval. <a href="/modelling/odr/odr-0002">ODR-0002</a>
    permits reconsideration only when a named external-vocabulary mapping, a named consumer
    and a recorded Council re-evaluation exist. If that gate later selects SSSOM, the scoped profile will need both concept
    identifiers, their source schemes and versions, and the review metadata needed by
    OPDA governance. It would cover internal mappings only if the decision explicitly says
    so; no such profile exists today. The <a href="/semantic-modelling/standards#standard-sssom">standards register</a>
    records this non-adoption boundary.
  </p>

  <h3 id="skos-predicates">Choose the SKOS predicate deliberately</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Predicate</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th><th scope="col">Review warning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>skos:exactMatch</code></th><td>The concepts can be used interchangeably across a wide range of information-retrieval applications. It is a subproperty of <code>skos:closeMatch</code>.</td><td>Symmetric and transitive: a bad decision can propagate through a chain. It is disjoint with <code>skos:broadMatch</code>, <code>skos:relatedMatch</code> and, by inverse/symmetry, <code>skos:narrowMatch</code>. Use rarely and never as a substitute for <code>owl:sameAs</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>skos:closeMatch</code></th><td>The concepts are similar enough to substitute in some information-retrieval applications.</td><td>Symmetric but deliberately not transitive.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>skos:broadMatch</code></th><td>The object concept is broader than the subject concept.</td><td>Direction matters; do not confuse it with the within-scheme <code>skos:broader</code> hierarchy.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>skos:narrowMatch</code></th><td>The object concept is narrower than the subject concept.</td><td>It is the inverse of <code>skos:broadMatch</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>skos:relatedMatch</code></th><td>The concepts have a useful symmetric associative relationship without hierarchy or equivalence.</td><td>State the use case; shared wording alone is not evidence.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3 id="mapping-workflow">From candidate comparison to governed mapping set</h3>
  <Diagram size="large" caption="This future mapping workflow keeps machine suggestions advisory. Affected semantic owners and Interoperability review definitions, examples, versions and identity criteria; only people choose a predicate. SSSOM remains outside the internal workflow unless a named external use case and Council decision select a profile whose scope explicitly covers the record.">
flowchart LR
  accTitle: Human-governed cross-context mapping workflow
  accDescr: Meanings from two contexts may produce an advisory candidate; evidence and human review determine a SKOS predicate, while any future SSSOM companion first requires an external use case and Council profile decision.
  MeaningA["Meaning in context A"]:::user
  MeaningB["Meaning in context B"]:::user
  Suggestion["Advisory candidate"]:::external
  Evidence["Definitions, examples and versions"]:::data
  Review["Semantic owners and Interoperability"]:::process
  Predicate["Reviewed mapping predicate"]:::service
  ProfileGate["Named external use case<br/>and Council profile gate"]:::security
  Record["Optional SSSOM record<br/>(not selected)"]:::external
  MappingSet["Versioned mapping set"]:::external
  MeaningA --> Suggestion
  MeaningB --> Suggestion
  Suggestion -.->|never auto-asserts| Review
  Evidence --> Review
  Review --> Predicate
  Review -.-> ProfileGate
  ProfileGate -.-> Record
  Predicate --> MappingSet
  Record -.-> MappingSet
  </Diagram>
  <ol>
    <li>Name both semantic owners, exact source versions and the consumer need.</li>
    <li>Let tools suggest candidates from labels, structure or usage, but machines must not assert mappings.</li>
    <li>Compare definitions, examples, counterexamples, identity criteria, logical shape and context.</li>
    <li>Choose reuse, Common-boundary contribution, a qualified predicate or an explicit no-map decision.</li>
    <li>Record justification, evidence, dissent, reviewer and status; do not use SSSOM for internal records unless the external-use-case and Council gates select a profile that explicitly covers them.</li>
    <li>Validate record completeness and, after recorded human disposition and separate publication authority, promote an immutable mapping-set version. Technical checks do not establish semantic equivalence.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="strength">Mapping strength and identity</h2>
  <p>
    A mapping record names its relation, direction, purpose, justification, confidence,
    owner, source/target versions and review status. Exact identity is rare. Use
    <code>owl:sameAs</code> only when two identifiers denote the same thing in every
    relevant interpretation. SKOS mapping relations can express close, exact, broad,
    narrow or related concept correspondences, but their use also requires evidence.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Choose sharing before mapping only when meaning is genuinely common</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Situation</th><th scope="col">Preferred treatment</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">A governed external term exactly fits</th><td>Reuse the pinned term-level profile.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Several contexts require one stable exchange meaning</th><td>Contribute a reviewed term to the small common boundary.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Contexts retain different but related meanings</th><td>Keep both semantic homes and record a qualified mapping.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Only a source field corresponds to a candidate construct</th><td>Record a coverage link, not an ontology identity mapping.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="receipt">Evidence receipt</h2>
  <p>
    A candidate receipt identifies source, version, permission, sensitivity, context
    owner, question, proposed change, affected semantic outputs, review record,
    disposition, standards-profile version and validation scope. A PDTF schema link must say
    whether it is evidence, corroboration, compatibility, comparison or supersession.
  </p>
  <p><a href="/semantic-modelling/validation">Next: validation, review and projections →</a></p>
</Layout>
